<section class="card allocation-connect"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">CONNECT YOUR PLAN TO REAL MONEY</p><h2>Give your next salary a destination.</h2></div><span class="badge">Only future income</span></div><p class="muted">Use this plan’s starting savings rate of <?= e(number_format($plan['savings_percent'],2)) ?>% and investment rate of <?= e(number_format($plan['investment_percent'],2)) ?>% for new manual or recurring income. Your investment share becomes a deposit in the asset you choose.</p>
<form class="form-grid" method="post" action="/salary/plan/allocations" data-confirm="Replace the current income allocation rules with this plan’s starting rates? Existing income will keep its original split."><?= csrf_field() ?><input type="hidden" name="month" value="<?= e($month) ?>"><label>Living & bills account<select name="spending_account_id" required><?php foreach ($allocationAccounts as $account): ?><option value="<?= e($account['id']) ?>"><?= e($account['name']) ?></option><?php endforeach; ?></select></label><label>Savings account<select name="savings_account_id" <?= $plan['savings_percent']>0 ? 'required' : '' ?>><option value="">Choose an account</option><?php foreach ($allocationSavingsAccounts as $account): ?><option value="<?= e($account['id']) ?>"><?= e($account['name']) ?></option><?php endforeach; ?></select></label><label>Investment asset<select name="investment_asset_id" <?= $plan['investment_percent']>0 ? 'required' : '' ?>><option value="">Choose an investment</option><?php foreach ($allocationAssets as $asset): ?><option value="<?= e($asset['id']) ?>"><?= e($asset['name']) ?></option><?php endforeach; ?></select></label><div><button>Use these allocation rules</button></div></form>
<p class="footnote">This replaces the current allocation rules and moves no money immediately. <a href="/investments">Add an investment asset</a> if your list is empty. Record salary in <a href="/salary">Money</a>, or set up <a href="/salary/recurring">recurring income</a>, to create actual savings and investment deposits. Review the suggested step-up each month before changing your rules.</p></section>
